here's the formula log base B of X  equals log base C of X over log base C  of B so what this means is you can pick  a new base base C okay and what you want  to remember is and it's easy to remember  that X goes in the numerator the B goes  in the denominator I always think the  B's a little bit lower so that's how you  can member goes in the denominator the X  is a little bit higher it goes in the  numerator I'll show you some examples  say for example you have log base 2 of  10 okay so we could write this as log  base 10 okay and then what we're gonna  do is we're gonna put the 10 in the  numerator we're gonna put the 2 in the  denominator we're gonna do log base 10  of 10 divided by log base 10 of 2 now  don't get confused this is not the same  thing as log base 10 of 5 you can't just  divide these and combine them into one  log you have to do these separately log  base 10 of 10 divided by log base 10 of  2 or another option is you could use  natural log so you could do natural log  of 1000 over natural log of 2 okay  these are all going to be equal to each  other it's just you're changing the base  I'll show you one more example let's say  you had log base 3 of 7 so what do we do  we do log base 10 of 7 over log base 10  of 3 7 goes in the numerator the 3 goes  in the denominator or or natural log of  7 over natural log of 3 exact same  answer it's just that some calculators  don't have the capacity to change the  base they just start restricted to  natural log the log base 10 so if you  have a calculator like this it's a quick  way to figure out approximately when you  do this on your calculator you can round  it to get an approximate answer of what  what this represents okay I'll see you  in the next video
 

